**1. Fan-out overstatement — YES, present identically.**
Look at `agg_matrix_financial_statement_line_table` (page 23-25). Same exact pattern we built: `debits` grouped, `credits` grouped, `summary_key` at journal grain, then `summary_key OUTER debits`, then `OUTER credits`. Same journal-grain join → same N×M row explosion when a journal has multiple debit_fs_lines × multiple credit_fs_lines. Summing `matrix_table_final.debit_amount`/`credit_amount` directly will overstate here too, same as ours.

**Proof this was already known to Deloitte's own devs:** they built `source_data_summary_compound` (page 24) — a plain `groupBy` per FS line, no cross-join — specifically as the reconciliation-safe counterpart to the fan-out matrix. That's exactly the `compound_summary` pattern I gave you. They hit the same problem and solved it the same way.

**2. LOB null-join bug — NOT present, because LOB doesn't exist in this script.**
Original has no LOB column anywhere. So that specific defect (both debit_fs_line/credit_fs_line null due to `null == null` join failure) can't trigger from LOB here.

**3. But the same *class* of bug can still trigger — via any null join key.**
Join keys are `journal_number`, `entity_id`, `is_standard`, `fiscal_year_identifier`.
- `is_standard` is null-safe — `apply_defaults()` coalesces it to `'N'`.
- `fiscal_year_identifier` is null-safe — hardcoded literal per branch.
- `journal_number` and `entity_id` are **not** defaulted anywhere in this script. If either is null in source GL rows, the same both-null matrix row symptom you saw in your screenshot will reproduce here, for the same reason — `null == null` fails in the outer joins.

**Recommendation if this runs against your data:** add a null-check / coalesce on `journal_number` and `entity_id` before `je_merged()`, same fix pattern as the LOB coalesce — cheap insurance against the identical defect class.
